  "critiquePairs": [
    {
      "id": "cp-1",
      "left": "strong",
      "right": "boolean-soup",
      "question": "Cancel is pressed halfway through a purchase. Which design can say, in one place, exactly what the machine is allowed to do at that moment? Click the class that holds that answer.",
      "targetsConcept": "state-modeling",
      "answer": {
        "design": "strong",
        "className": "MachineState"
      },
      "telling": "On the left, the machine's phase is a class, and each phase says what it allows. Cancel during HasMoney is one method on one class. On the right, the phase is three booleans that have to agree — hasMoney, isDispensing, isSoldOut — and every method has to check the right combination. That is the boolean-soup fault: the state exists, but it is spread across flags nobody can see together."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp-2",
      "left": "money-as-int",
      "right": "strong",
      "question": "The coin store has 50 in it but cannot make 30 in change. In which design is that a fact the code can even express? Click the class that makes it expressible.",
      "targetsConcept": "encapsulation",
      "answer": {
        "design": "strong",
        "className": "Money"
      },
      "telling": "An integer balance knows the amount and nothing else. Exact change is about denominations: having 50 as one coin is not the same as having five tens. The right design models Money as a bag of coins, so the change-maker can look at what is actually there and refuse before dispensing. The left design does the subtraction and hopes the coins exist — which is the silent failure the sixth requirement is about."
    }
  ],
